From 0cb394c2dc9e25ea5f854d351d81b5c11bc2e602 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dfattal <dfattal@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 20 Aug 2026 13:27:30 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] inline-3d(#94): an offscreen registered tile is an expected
 miss, not a fallback
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

On the gallery, one element (DOMNodeId 4) logged
"inline-3D ID-MATCH FALLBACK: reason=layer_id stamped but no root quad
carries it" on EVERY frame, forever, and then fell through to the geometric
matcher. It is not an anomaly and it never was: it is a lazily-registered
inline-3D tile sitting off the viewport, and the id-match miss is structural.

The two halves of the Phase-1 identity join are collected from DIFFERENT
layer lists, and only one of them is viewport-gated.

  * The RECTS come from LayerTreeHostImpl::CollectTrackedElementRects, which
    walks `base::Reversed(*active_tree())` — the WHOLE active tree, drawn or
    not. For kInline3dWeave, CalculateVisibleBounds is called with
    use_unclipped_element_bounds=true, which deliberately DISCARDS the layer's
    visible_layer_rect (viewport-clipped, and EMPTY for an offscreen layer) in
    favour of the raw element bounds — that carve-out exists so an undecorated
    canvas's zero-visible background chunk does not zero the weave rect, and
    its side effect is that a scrolled-off tile still reports a non-empty rect.
    The rect carries layer->stable_id_for_shared_quad_state(), which is
    per-LayerImpl-instance and always > 0, whether or not the layer draws.

  * The QUADS come from CalculateRenderPasses, which iterates
    EffectTreeLayerListIterator — the DRAWN layer list only. An offscreen layer
    is never visited, never calls AppendQuads, never creates a SharedQuadState.
    (Even if it were visited, TextureLayerImpl::AppendQuads early-returns on an
    empty visible_quad_rect before creating the quad.)

So the rect arrives stamped with a layer id that NO root resource quad can
ever carry. The SDK's rootMargin prefetch keeps exactly such a tile alive and
registered while it is outside the viewport, which is why the miss repeats
every frame for the same element rather than flickering like real churn.

The fix is the one the issue proposed: recognise the case and say nothing.
Compute the root pass's own footprint in root-target space — the same
MapEnclosedRectWith2dAxisAlignedTransform(root_surface_transform_,
gfx::Rect(viewport_bounds)) idiom already used for the colour-usage full-damage
rect, and the same space both the tracked rects and root_resource_quads_ live
in — and when a tracked rect does not intersect it, skip the element for this
frame. No matcher change: id_match and best_match are untouched, and every
on-viewport rect takes exactly the path it took before.

Skipping best_match for these rects is not merely quieter, it is safer. The
element contributed no quad BY CONSTRUCTION, so any quad that clears the 70%
overlap bar against an off-viewport target belongs to a DIFFERENT element, and
adopting it would (a) put that stranger's ResourceId into the suppression set
Display::DrawAndSwap hands the renderer — punching an on-screen hole the weave
never fills, the exact failure web#12 named "never punch a hole you cannot
fill" — and (b) hand the weave that stranger's pixels for this token. The tile
loses nothing in exchange: it is off the viewport, so its mailbox is not drawn
this frame either way. (Bar-clearing is unlikely, not impossible — it needs a
quad of comparable area sitting mostly offscreen on top of the target, which a
fast scroll can produce — so this is a latent hole-punch removed, not a
theoretical one.)

The rect itself still goes to the weave in frame.inline_3d_rects: this touches
only the canvas-resource join, so the index-keyed provider contract and the
per-token GPU state are unchanged.

The class stays countable, on its own marker ("inline-3D offscreen tile") and a
~30 s throttle, so that grepping for "ID-MATCH FALLBACK" — the diagnostic
recipe in the issue — now reports only the genuinely anomalous case: an
ON-viewport rect whose stamped layer contributes no root quad.

Refs: DisplayXR/displayxr-browser#94
---
 .../viz/service/display/surface_aggregator.cc | 52 +++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 52 insertions(+)

diff --git a/components/viz/service/display/surface_aggregator.cc b/components/viz/service/display/surface_aggregator.cc
index 18cc0ab..cbf38ef 100644
--- a/components/viz/service/display/surface_aggregator.cc
+++ b/components/viz/service/display/surface_aggregator.cc
@@ -2558,11 +2558,63 @@ AggregatedFrame SurfaceAggregator::Aggregate(
     frame.inline_3d_rects.push_back(
         {r.rect, r.id, r.sqs_layer_id, r.layer_namespace_id});
   }
+  // browser#94: the root pass's own footprint, expressed in the SAME
+  // root-target space as both the tracked rects and |root_resource_quads_|
+  // (identical idiom to the color-usage full-damage rect above). A tracked rect
+  // that does not touch this rect belongs to an element that is entirely off
+  // the viewport this frame.
+  const gfx::Rect root_output_rect =
+      cc::MathUtil::MapEnclosedRectWith2dAxisAlignedTransform(
+          root_surface_transform_, gfx::Rect(viewport_bounds));
   // Phase-1 identity: match each inline-3d canvas rect to the resource quad
   // that draws it by IDENTITY first, geometry only as a fallback.
   for (const Inline3dTrackedRect& canvas : inline_3d_rects_) {
     std::optional<RootResourceQuad> m = id_match(canvas, root_resource_quads_);
     if (!m) {
+      // browser#94 — the OFFSCREEN-BUT-REGISTERED tile: an expected absence,
+      // not an anomaly, and neither the log nor the geometric fallback below
+      // may fire for it.
+      //
+      // The two halves of this join are collected from DIFFERENT layer lists.
+      // The rects come from LayerTreeHostImpl::CollectTrackedElementRects,
+      // which walks the WHOLE active tree, and for kInline3dWeave
+      // CalculateVisibleBounds deliberately discards the layer's
+      // (viewport-clipped, and for an offscreen layer EMPTY)
+      // visible_layer_rect in favour of the raw unclipped element bounds — so a
+      // scrolled-off tile still reports a non-empty rect, carrying the layer id
+      // stamped from stable_id_for_shared_quad_state(), which is per-instance
+      // and non-zero whether or not the layer draws. The quads come from
+      // CalculateRenderPasses, which walks only the DRAWN layer list; an
+      // offscreen layer is never visited, emits no SharedQuadState, and
+      // therefore no root resource quad can ever carry its id. The SDK's
+      // rootMargin prefetch keeps exactly such a tile live and registered while
+      // it sits outside the viewport, so the miss repeats every frame for the
+      // same element — the signature reported on the gallery.
+      //
+      // Skipping best_match here is not merely quieter, it is safer. This
+      // element contributed no quad by construction, so any quad clearing the
+      // overlap bar against an off-viewport target is a DIFFERENT element's,
+      // and adopting it would (a) add that stranger's resource to the
+      // suppression set in Display::DrawAndSwap, punching an on-screen hole the
+      // weave never fills, and (b) hand the weave the wrong pixels for this
+      // token. The tile itself loses nothing: it is off the viewport, so its
+      // mailbox is never drawn.
+      //
+      // Deliberately still countable, but on its own marker and a ~30 s throttle
+      // so `grep "ID-MATCH FALLBACK"` — the issue's diagnostic recipe — reports
+      // only genuine anomalies (an ON-viewport rect with no quad).
+      if (!canvas.rect.Intersects(root_output_rect)) {
+        static int offscreen_dbg = 0;
+        if ((offscreen_dbg++ % 1800) == 0) {
+          VLOG(1) << "[DisplayXR] inline-3D offscreen tile: id="
+                  << canvas.id.ToString()
+                  << " layer_id=" << canvas.sqs_layer_id
+                  << " target=" << canvas.rect.ToString() << " outside root "
+                  << root_output_rect.ToString()
+                  << " — registered but not composited; no quad expected";
+        }
+        continue;
+      }
       // Log WHY the exact join could not be used — the two reasons need
       // different follow-ups, and telling them apart from a log is the whole
       // value of the marker. Throttled: a persistent fallback would otherwise
